ues and recover, while a linear increase would still probe the network too frequently during severe congestion. Additionally, exponential growth means fewer total retransmission attempts are made during a congestion episode, reducing offered load. A linear increase risks keeping the network in a congested state by not backing off aggressively enough when congestion is severe. | (d) Flow control is an end-to-end mechanism that prevents the sender from overwhelming the receiver's buffer. The receiver advertises a receive window (rwnd) in TCP ACK headers indicating how much buffer space it has available; the sender limits unacknowledged data to this value. Congestion control is a mechanism to prevent the sender from overwhelming the network (routers/links), not the receiver. The sender maintains a congestion window (cwnd) and infers network congestion from packet loss events (timeouts or duplicate ACKs). Algorithms include: slow start (exponential cwnd growth), congestion avoidance (linear/AIMD growth), and fast retransmit/recovery. The effective sending rate is governed by min(rwnd, cwnd). Key distinction: flow control is receiver-driven (explicit signalling via window field), congestion control is sender-driven (implicit inference from network behaviour). The sliding window combines both: the window size is min(rwnd, cwnd), and the sender may only have that many bytes unacknowledged at any time, sliding forward as ACKs arrive. | (e) (i) Max-min fairness works by iteratively allocating bandwidth equally until a flow's demand is satisfied, then redistributing remaining capacity. Step 1: Divide 200 Mbps equally: 200/3 \u2248 66.67 Mbps each. f1 only wants 60 Mbps, so f1 is satisfied at 60 Mbps. Remaining: 200-60 = 140 Mbps for f2 and f3. Step 2: Divide 140 Mbps equally between f2 and f3: 70 Mbps each. f2 only wants 80 Mbps, so 70 < 80, f2 gets 70 Mbps. f3 gets 70 Mbps. Wait - re-check: 140/2 = 70. f2 wants 80 but only gets 70, so f2 is bottlenecked. Final allocation: f1=60 Mbps, f2=70 Mbps, f3=70 Mbps. (ii) To cheat max-min fairness, f3 could split its traffic into multiple sub-flows (e.g., open multiple TCP connections). Each sub-flow appears as an independent flow, so with more flows f3 claims a larger share of the bandwidth at each iteration of the max-min algorithm, effectively increasing its total allocated bandwidth beyond 70 Mbps.",
