    "model_solution": "(a) A neighbour discovery protocol should include: (1) HELLO messages broadcast periodically on each interface containing fields such as: [message_type | sender_node_id | interface_id | timestamp | hello_interval | hold_time | optional: IP/MAC address]. (2) Each node broadcasts HELLO messages on all its links at a defined interval (e.g. every 10s). (3) A neighbour table is maintained: upon receiving a HELLO, the node records the sender_id and interface on which it arrived as a neighbour. (4) A hold timer (typically 3x hello interval) is reset on each received HELLO; if it expires, the neighbour is declared dead and removed. (5) Optionally, bidirectionality can be confirmed by including a neighbour list in the HELLO so each side can verify the other has heard them (as in OSPF). This gives each node a list of directly connected neighbours and link costs/states for use by the routing algorithm. | (b) A proper answer should define Link-State Routing adapted for a LEO satellite network: (1) **Message formats**: LSA packet containing {source_node_id, sequence_number, TTL, timestamp, list of (neighbour_id, link_cost, link_state)} \u2014 for satellites, link_cost could include RTT and remaining visibility window. (2) **Flooding mechanism**: each node forwards an LSA out all interfaces except the one it arrived on; sequence numbers prevent re-processing; TTL bounds propagation. (3) **Topology database**: each node maintains a full graph; Dijkstra computes shortest paths. (4) **Satellite-specific extensions**: LSAs should include orbital parameters or predicted link-up/link-down times so nodes can pre-compute future topologies; links are annotated with validity windows. (5) **Update triggers**: new LSA on neighbour appearance/disappearance (predictable from orbital mechanics) or periodic refresh. (6) **Ground stations**: treated as ordinary nodes with fixed positions, issuing LSAs like any other node. | (c) In link-state routing, each of the N nodes broadcasts a link-state packet (LSP) describing its local links. Each node has some degree d_i (number of links). Each LSP from node i contains d_i entries and must be received by all N nodes. The total non-redundant information is the sum over all nodes i of (d_i \u00d7 N), i.e. N \u00d7 \u03a3d_i = N \u00d7 2E, where E is the number of edges (since each edge appears in two nodes' LSPs). So the bound is O(N\u00b7E) or more precisely 2NE link entries. Equivalently, since \u03a3d_i = 2E, the total non-redundant information is proportional to N\u00b7E. | (d) For a network layer routing problem involving channel bandwidth B and speed of light c = 3\u00d710\u2078 m/s with zero per-node processing time, the solution would typically involve computing end-to-end latency components. Transmission delay = L/B (where L is packet size in bits), propagation delay = d/c (where d is physical distance between nodes). Total latency for a path of k hops with distances d\u2081...d\u2096 = (k \u00d7 L/B) + (\u03a3d\u1d62)/c. If store-and-forward switching is assumed, each intermediate node must fully receive the packet before forwarding, giving k\u00d7(L/B) transmission delay plus total propagation delay. With cut-through switching, transmission delay reduces to L/B (only the final hop) plus propagation. Optimal routing would minimise this total delay metric. If the question concerns routing table computation (e.g. Dijkstra or Bellman-Ford), the answer would detail the algorithm steps using these delay values as link costs. | (d(i)) For a satellite constellation of N nodes, worst-case propagation of routing information (e.g. via distance-vector or link-state flooding) traverses the diameter of the network. With inter-satellite distances of order 1000\u20135000 km and speed of light ~3\u00d710^8 m/s, per-hop propagation delay is roughly 3\u201317 ms. For a constellation of ~60 satellites arranged in a ring-like topology, the diameter might be ~30 hops, giving propagation delay of order 0.1