    "model_solution": "(a) Flow control is an end-to-end mechanism between sender and receiver (e.g., TCP receive window) that prevents the sender from overwhelming the receiver's buffer capacity \u2014 it is receiver-driven. Congestion control (e.g., TCP AIMD, slow start) is a network-wide concern preventing routers/links from being overwhelmed \u2014 it infers network state from packet loss and RTT signals. A stable end-to-end latency (low jitter) is crucial because: (1) TCP congestion control relies on RTT measurements to set retransmission timers (RTO) and infer congestion \u2014 stable latency means RTT estimates are accurate, so the cwnd is managed correctly; (2) in wireless networks, channel fading, MAC-layer retransmissions, and handoffs introduce latency spikes and jitter, causing TCP to falsely detect congestion (e.g., timeout triggers slow start unnecessarily); (3) stable latency also means flow control windows can be sized appropriately \u2014 the bandwidth-delay product is predictable, so sender/receiver buffers are neither wastefully large nor bottlenecking throughput; (4) for mobile networks specifically, handoffs cause latency spikes that disrupt both control loops simultaneously. | (b) The core challenge is that mobile endpoints change IP address as they move between networks (e.g. switching cell towers or WiFi access points). Since TCP connections are identified by a 4-tuple (src IP, src port, dst IP, dst port), a change in IP address breaks any existing TCP connection, disrupting connection-based applications. A good solution discussed without reworking end-system applications: **Mobile IP / proxy-based indirection**. For example, with a mobile proxy (or MPTCP at OS level), a home agent or proxy retains a stable IP address visible to the remote server. When the mobile device moves and gets a new IP (care-of address), it registers this new address with the home agent. The home agent tunnels traffic between the server (which sees only the stable address) and the mobile device's new IP. The end-system application on both sides sees no change \u2014 it communicates with the same IP as before. Another approach is application-layer proxies (e.g. SOCKS proxy or a session border controller in VoIP) that maintain session state and re-establish underlying connections transparently. MPTCP at the OS/kernel level also allows connections to survive IP changes without modifying applications. A concrete example: a voice call over a mobile network \u2014 as the user moves, a session proxy maintains the call session identifier while re-establishing the underlying IP path, so neither the user's app nor the remote server need modification. | (c) A reverse proxy like Varnish caches static snapshots of dynamic web pages and serves them to clients. Primary use-case: a high-traffic website (e.g., a news site or e-commerce platform) can serve cached pages to thousands of simultaneous users without each request hitting the origin/backend server, dramatically reducing load, improving response times, and enabling the site to scale beyond the backend's raw capacity. For example, during a viral news story, the cached front page can be served to millions of users while the backend database handles only periodic cache refresh requests. When to disable: the reverse proxy cache should be disabled (or cache TTL set to zero) for content that is highly time-sensitive or must be personalised/correct at the moment of viewing - e.g., live stock prices, auction countdown pages, personalised user dashboards, shopping cart contents, or pages showing inventory levels. Serving a stale cached snapshot in these cases could cause real harm (e.g., a user sees an item as 'in stock' from a cached page when it sold out seconds ago). It should also be bypassed for authenticated/session-specific pages where different users must see different content. | (d) Scale: With 500M daily users, 500M posts, and 100M new images/day averaging 100KB, the working set is ~10TB/day of new content alone, far exceeding what can be cached without careful prioritisation. Systems use multi-tier CDN caching (edge \u2192 regional \u2192 origin). The heavy-tailed distribution means ~1% of images may account for ~50%+ of requests; these hot images must be identified and aggressively cached at edge nodes, while long-tail images may only be cached at origin or regional level. Challenges include: dynamically identifying trending content (hashtags, virality), TTL management (balancing freshness vs. cache efficiency), geographic distribution of caches, and consistency of metadata (likes, tags). After the 2021 reboot (Facebook BGP outage), all caches were cold. Cache warming requires real traffic to repopulate them \u2014 for weeks, requests miss the cache and hit origin servers, which become overloaded, causing poor performance. The system cannot instantly know which images are currently popular (the popularity model must be rebuilt from scratch), so it may serve previously cached or stale content that was popular before the outage but is no longer relevant. Irrelevant images appear because old cached entries (with long TTLs or reconstructed from persistent storage) reflect pre-outage popularity distributions, not current user interests. The heavy-tail means most traffic converges on a small hot set \u2014 until that hot set is re-identified and cached, performance degrades disproportionately across the whole system.",
