    "model_solution": "(a) Three significant differences between DPLL and resolution include: (1) **Logic handled**: DPLL is a propositional method, while resolution (with unification) handles first-order logic. (2) **Procedure type**: DPLL is a model-construction/search procedure that attempts to build a satisfying assignment by case splitting and unit propagation, whereas resolution is a refutation procedure that derives the empty clause $\\square$ to prove unsatisfiability. (3) **Termination/completeness**: DPLL always terminates and is a decision procedure for propositional SAT; resolution is refutation-complete but may not terminate on satisfiable FOL inputs. A fourth notable difference is that DPLL simplifies the clause set (deleting clauses), while resolution generates new clauses from existing ones, and all derived clauses may be reused. | (b) (i) **Resolution theorem proving**: Pure literal elimination is generally *not applicable* to resolution. Pure literal elimination works by identifying literals that appear with only one polarity (positive or negative) across all clauses and assigning them values to satisfy all clauses containing them. Resolution, however, works on the full clause set and derives new clauses by resolving complementary literals \u2014 it needs to consider both polarities of a literal to resolve them. Removing pure literals would be unsound in the resolution context, since resolution operates on an *unsatisfiable* clause set (refutation procedure) and may need those literals to derive the empty clause $\\square$. The concept simply does not fit into the resolution framework. (ii) **Prolog**: Pure literal elimination is also *not directly applicable* to Prolog. Prolog uses SLD-resolution (a goal-directed, top-down strategy) and only resolves against the head of clauses. A 'pure' positive literal (a predicate appearing only in clause heads, never in goals) would never be queried, but Prolog does not systematically detect and eliminate such