    "feedback": "(a) Strong answer covering all key points. The student correctly identifies the power wall as the breakdown of Dennard scaling \u2014 specifically that supply voltage can no longer be reduced proportionally as transistors shrink because leakage current becomes too significant. They correctly connect this to the consequence that power and heat grow unmanageably with increased clock frequency. The definition of dark silicon is accurate: not all transistors can be powered/run simultaneously at full speed without exceeding thermal/power limits, so portions of the chip must remain powered down ('dark') at any given time. The phrase 'dark silicon is the unused area' is a slight simplification (it's more precisely the area that must remain powered off to stay within thermal budget, not merely unused), but the conceptual understanding is clearly present and the answer earns full marks. | (b) Good overall understanding of both concepts demonstrated. Race-to-dark is correctly described as running the workload as fast as possible then powering down the hardware, and the student correctly identifies this saves power through minimising idle/leakage time. The DVFS description is broadly correct \u2014 adjusting voltage and frequency during execution trades performance for lower power consumption. The final observation that DVFS can sustain lower power over a longer period (rather than short bursts) is a nice contrast that shows genuine understanding of the distinction. However, the answer is missing a key quantitative/physical insight that makes race-to-dark work: since dynamic power scales as $P \\propto CV^2f$, finishing work quickly and then cutting power (including leakage) can yield better energy savings than running slowly at lower voltage, *because leakage power is constant regardless of frequency*. The student also doesn't explicitly note that race-to-dark keeps voltage/frequency fixed at maximum, whereas DVFS modulates them \u2014 this is the most direct