    "feedback": "(a) The answer has some correct observations but also contains a significant error and lacks precision in several areas.\n\n**What's correct:**\n- Correctly identifies that signal() increments the semaphore counter.\n- Correctly notes that notify wakes a waiting thread.\n- Correctly identifies that notify can have no effect if no thread is waiting.\n- Neither is necessarily a blocking primitive \u2014 this is correctly implied.\n\n**Key error:**\n- The claim that 'thread suspends immediately and signalled thread resumes with the condition guaranteed true' is **wrong** about signal(). signal() on a semaphore does *not* cause the calling thread to suspend. The calling thread continues running. This is a fundamental confusion \u2014 it conflates signal() with something like a 'signal and wait' or Mesa vs Hoare semantics confusion, but even under Hoare semantics this applies to condition variable signal, not semaphore signal.\n\n**Missing distinctions:**\n- The key *commonality* is not clearly stated: both are used to wake/unblock threads that are waiting.\n- The key *difference* is not well articulated: signal() on a semaphore has memory (the counter is incremented even if no thread is waiting, so a future wait() will not block), whereas notify() on a condition variable is *not* remembered \u2014 if no thread is waiting, the notification is lost entirely. This is the most important distinction and is absent.\n- The answer says signal 'can have no effect' when the counter is high \u2014 this is confused reasoning. Incrementing the counter always has an effect on the counter value; it just doesn't immediately unblock anyone. A future thread doing wait() will benefit. This is actually the opposite of notify's behaviour.\n- Should mention that neither blocks the *calling* thread more explicitly and cleanly. | (b(i)) The student has the right intuition but the definition is imprecise in two ways. First, 'some transaction' at the end should be 'all transacti