    "model_solution": "(a) **In common:** Both signal() and notify() are used to wake up one or more threads that are blocked waiting on the respective synchronisation primitive. Neither is a blocking primitive \u2014 the calling thread does not suspend; it continues execution after the call.\n\n**Differences:** The critical difference lies in whether the operation has memory:\n- **signal()** on a semaphore increments the internal counter. This increment is *remembered*: even if no thread is currently waiting, a future thread calling wait() will find the counter positive and proceed without blocking. Thus signal() never truly 'has no effect' \u2014 it always modifies the counter.\n- **notify()** on a condition variable wakes one waiting thread (if any). Crucially, it has *no memory*: if no thread is currently waiting on the condition variable, the notification is **lost entirely**, and a future thread calling wait() will block regardless. Therefore notify() **can have no effect** if no thread is waiting.\n\n**Blocking:** Neither is a blocking primitive for the *calling* thread. signal() and notify() return immediately.\n\n**No effect:** notify() can have no effect (lost wakeup if no waiters). signal() always modifies the semaphore counter, though it may not immediately unblock any thread if none are waiting \u2014 but the effect is preserved for future waiters. | (b(i)) A set of concurrently executing transactions is serialisable if the outcome (i.e. the resulting state of shared data) is equivalent to the outcome that would be produced by some serial execution of those same transactions \u2014 that is, some ordering in which the transactions run one at a time, without interleaving. Serialisability is the standard correctness criterion for transactional concurrency: it guarantees that concurrent execution does not produce any result that could not have arisen from a purely sequential schedule. | (b(ii)) A **conflict** between two threads (transactions) occurs when two operations from different transactions access the **same data item**, and at least one of the operations is a **write**. There are three cases: read-write (RW), write-read (WR), and write-write (WW) conflicts. Two read operations on the same item do *not* conflict. Conflicts impose an ordering constraint: the conflicting operations must occur in one order or the other, and swapping them would change the observable result.\n\nConflicts can be used to check for **conflict-serialisability** via a **conflict/precedence graph**: create a node per transaction; draw a directed edge $T_i \\to T_j$ whenever $T_i$ has an operation that conflicts with a later operation of $T_j$. The schedule is conflict-serialisable if and only if this graph is **acyclic** (a DAG), because a topological ordering of the DAG gives a serial schedule equivalent to the concurrent one. A cycle would imply a circular ordering requirement, making serialisation impossible. Note that conflict-serialisability is a *sufficient* condition for serialisability but not necessary \u2014 some serialisable schedules may be rejected by this test. | (b(iii)) **Advantage:** Non-strict isolation allows a transaction to read values written by other transactions that have not yet committed. This avoids blocking reads until a writing transaction commits, improving throughput and reducing response times \u2014 more transactions can proceed concurrently.\n\n**Disadvantage:** If a transaction reads an uncommitted value and the writing transaction subsequently aborts, the reading transaction has used a value that was never validly committed ('dirty read'). This can lead to incorrect results, cascading aborts, lost updates, or data corruption. Recovery becomes more complex because aborting one transaction may require aborting others that read its dirty data. | (b(iv)) Increment and decrement operations are typically decomposed into a read followed by a write ($x \\leftarrow x+1$). Under standard conflict analysis, two concurrent increments would produce a read-write or write-write conflict and be flagged as potentially non-serialisable. However, if increment and decrement are treated as indivisible *composite* operations, conflict analysis can exploit their **commutativity**: increment followed by increment gives the same result regardless of order, so they do not genuinely conflict. Similarly, decrement-decrement pairs do not conflict. Only an increment paired with a decrement (or with a plain read/write) need be flagged. By recognising these operations at a higher semantic level, conflict analysis reports fewer spurious conflicts, allowing more interleavings to be permitted, and thus improving performance (more transactions can proceed concurrently without unnecessary serialisation or abort). | (b(v)) A good answer would identify that simplistic conflict analysis flags any two accesses to the same variable where at least one is a write as a conflict, and then checks the conflict graph for cycles to determine non-serialisability. However, this can be a false alarm. For example, consider two threads both reading and writing a variable $x$ where the combined effect is commutative or idempotent \u2014 e.g., both threads assign the same constant value: `x = 5`. Simplistic conflict analysis would report a write-write conflict, but since both writes produce the same result regardless of ordering, the outcome is always equivalent to a serial execution and there is no real problem. Another classic example: two threads each set $x$ to a fixed value (e.g. $x := 0$), creating a write-write conflict in the analysis, but since the result is identical under any ordering, the execution is effectively serialisable. The key point is that simplistic conflict analysis is purely syntactic \u2014 it does not consider the *values* being written or the semantic commutativity of operations. | (c) ",
